David Bianco's Pyramid of Pain model

The important shift is from “What IOC's should we block?” to “What is the attacker trying to accomplish, and how do we detect the behavior even after they change their infrastructure?”


Why Pure IOC Matching FailsShort Lifespan & High Churn: Attackers rotate IP addresses, domains, and file hashes automatically in CI/CD-style attack infrastructure. Hash values are trivial to change; forcing an adversary to change an IP takes seconds.Reactive Stance: Blocklists only help after someone else (or you) has already been hit. They offer zero coverage against zero-days, novel tradecraft, or identity-based attacks.Alert Fatigue: Ingesting millions of low-confidence IOC feeds swamps security operations centers (SOCs) with false positives, obscuring real signal.Where CTI Focus Is Shifting1. Behavior & TTPs (Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures)Focusing on how adversaries operate via frameworks like MITRE ATT&CK makes defense exponentially more durable. An attacker can swap an IP in seconds, but changing their core post-exploitation playbook, credential-dumping methods, or lateral movement techniques requires substantial retraining and re-tooling.2. Threat-Informed Defense & EmulationInstead of passively ingesting feeds, mature CTI teams use intelligence to drive active testing:Adversary Emulation: Simulating specific threat actor groups known to target your industry to find coverage gaps before an actual breach occurs.Detections-as-Code: Translating TTP intelligence directly into behavioral detection rules (e.g., Sigma, YARA-L) targeted at high-risk behavior rather than static indicators.3. Context & Strategic IntelligenceStrategic CTI shifts the focus from “What binary ran?” to:Intent & Capability: Who is targeting our sector, and what are their primary objectives?Supply Chain & Surface Exposure: Where are our systemic vulnerabilities across vendor ecosystems and external attack surfaces?Prioritization: Guiding vulnerability management so teams patch bugs actively being exploited in the wild rather than chasing pure CVSS scores.Key TakeawayTreat IOCs as table stakes for automated, short-term tactical blocking, but anchor your long-term CTI strategy in adversary behavior, threat modeling, and proactive detection engineering.

