In terms of CTI specifically, there are different forms of structure that have become prevalent over the last few years, so let’s take a look at some of the most common and some example use cases for them.

STIX, or Structured Threat Information Expression
STIX forces relationships between data. You no longer have to look exclusively at IOCs or TTPs, you can look at relationships between reports, threat actors, tools, Malware, vulnerabilities, etc. STIX covers the whole threat and not just a subset.

STIX is designed to make intelligence sharing easy. By being the standard for which intelligence is shared, you can easily share intelligence with different systems and organizations and be confident the end recipient will see the data as you intend it to be seen.

By its nature, STIX enables link analysis of data for human analysts. It can utilize graph technology to show the data and allow analysts to walk between different entities and identify new links or leads that you may otherwise miss.

STIX entities support the use of STIX Cyber Observables (SCOs) (formerly CyBOX). These observables allow link analysis but also provide links between disparate datasets and enrichments.
